/-!
# The joint tuple component of the incidence ring

For tuples `x`, `y`, `z` in a field `Ω` over `k`, evaluation at the joint tuple `(x, y, z)` kills
the incidence ideal `I(x, y) + I(y, z)` of `k[X, Y, Z]` (`locusIncidence_le_idealOf`), so it
factors through the incidence ring of `exists_locusIncidence_algEquiv`. If every coordinate of `z`
is algebraic over the field generated by `y`, one algebraicity clause of (8.1), then the kernel of
this evaluation is a minimal prime of the incidence ring, and its zero locus has dense image in
`Spec (k[Y] ⧸ I(y))` along the map induced by the inclusion of the variables `Y`
(`locusIncidence_jointPrime_dominating`). No condition on `x` is needed.

Conversely, when `α` and `γ` are finite, the coordinates of `x` and `z` are algebraic over the field
generated by `y` and `Ω` is algebraically closed, every such component arises in this way:
`locusIncidence_dominatingComponents_eq_jointLoci` identifies the components with dense middle
image exactly with the zero loci of the images of the vanishing ideals of the joint tuples
`(x', y, z')` whose pairs `(x', y)` and `(y, z')` have the loci of `(x, y)` and `(y, z)`. The new
endpoints are the images of `x` and `z` under embeddings of the function fields fixing `k(y)`, which
realize the minimal primes of the function-field tensor product
(`exists_productMap_ker_of_isPrime`).

The kernel is the contraction of the kernel of the multiplication map
`k(x, y) ⊗[k(y)] k(y, z) → Ω` along the map of `exists_locusIncidence_dominatingComponents`. That
kernel is a minimal prime in the algebraic case of `locusIncidence_jointPrime_dominating` because
the tensor product is integral over `k(x, y)` and the kernel meets `k(x, y)` trivially.

For finite tuples with `rk(x, y, z) + rk(y) = rk(x, y) + rk(y, z)`, ranks being transcendence
degrees over `k`, the same conclusions hold without assuming either outer tuple algebraic over
`k(y)` (`locusIncidence_jointPrime_dominating_of_rank`). Moreover the contraction of the kernel
to the endpoint variables is the locus of `(x, z)`, and the coordinate ring of the component has
Krull dimension equal to the rank of `(x, y, z)`. A maximal subfamily `u` of `x` algebraically
independent over `k(y)` stays independent over `k(y, z)` by the rank equation. Hence
`k(y)(u) ⊗[k(y)] k(y, z)` embeds in `Ω` by linear disjointness (`isGeneric_baseChange`), the
function-field tensor product is integral over it, and the kernel meets it trivially. This is the
generic-component step of the source's (8.4c) and (8.6): the given tuple is a generic point of the
fiber product, its component projects to the locus of the endpoints, and its dimension is the
rank. The identification of that endpoint locus with a member of a parameter family is a separate
obligation.

This is the generic-point clause of the geometric fiber-product bridge of blueprint §8.1 (8.4c,
8.6) (#5). `TupleComposition` proves relational associativity of finite-tuple germs, nonempty
composition for equal middle ideals, and the equality between composite carrier ideals and
endpoint contractions of these components, in an algebraically closed ambient field.
`TupleCompositionField` derives target rank one and proves finiteness of the actual generated
joint field over the endpoint field and over both end fields, under the relevant finite-index
and algebraicity hypotheses. These field results work over arbitrary base and ambient fields.
`LocusComponentField` identifies each actual middle-dominating component's coordinate ring and
fraction field with the realized joint generated ring and compositum, including polynomial
representative formulas. The remaining source obligations are recorded in the book's hard-kernel
chapter and the current frontier of `PROJECT_GOAL.md`.

This module is part of the formalization of the Evans–Hrushovski–Gismatullin
reconstruction theorem; the source of truth is `sources/blueprint.tex`.

**Status:** proved (#5, C2 support).
-/


